	defer cancel()

	if err := srv.Shutdown(ctx); err != nil {
		log.Fatalf("graceful shutdown failed: %v", err)
	}
	log.Println("server stopped")
}

When does the header appear?

`http.Server.Shutdown` stops accepting new connections and then drains existing ones. During the drain phase, Go sets `Connection: close` on the final response of each in-flight connection so the client knows the server will not reuse that socket. The header is emitted only on responses served after `Shutdown` is invoked; responses completed before the call carry the usual keep-alive semantics. Because the TCP FIN is flushed immediately after the last body byte is written, the client observes the close with no additional round-trip beyond the response itself.

