Here's a compact Rust CLI that reads JSON lines from stdin, accumulates them into batches of 100, and flushes each batch (plus any remainder at EOF) to stdout as a JSON array:

```rust
use std::io::{self, BufRead, Write};

const BATCH_SIZE: usize = 100;

fn main() -> io::Result<()> {
    let stdin = io::stdin();
    let mut reader = stdin.lock();
    let mut out = io::stdout();
    let mut batch: Vec<String> = Vec::new();

    loop {
        let mut line = String::new();
        let n = reader.read_line(&mut line)?;
        if n == 0 {
            break; // EOF
        }
        let line = line.trim_end();
        if line.is_empty() {
            continue;
        }
        // Validate that the line is well-formed JSON
        serde_json::from_str::<serde_json::Value>(line)
            .expect("invalid JSON line on stdin");
        batch.push(line.to_string());

        if batch.len() > BATCH_SIZE {
            flush_batch(&mut out, &batch)?;
            batch.clear();
        }
    }

    // Flush any remaining lines at EOF
    if !batch.is_empty() {
        flush_batch(&mut out, &batch)?;
    }

    Ok(())
}

fn flush_batch(out: &mut impl Write, batch: &[String]) -> io::Result<()> {
    let payload = serde_json::to_string(batch)?;
    writeln!(out, "{}", payload)?;
    out.flush()?;
    Ok(())
}
```

Usage: `cat events.jsonl | cargo run --release`.

Each batch is emitted as a single JSON array on one line, so downstream consumers can parse it with a standard JSON decoder. The `expect` on `serde_json::from_str` keeps the hot path allocation-free for the common valid-input case; swap it for a `match` if you want graceful per-line error reporting.

